    [
     3,
     "\r<br>&gt; On Aug 30, 2015, at 11:41 PM, Lei Zhang &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">bnuzhan...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; any feedback or comments?\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; On Thursday, August 27, 2015 at 9:42:22 PM UTC+2, Lei Zhang wrote:\r<br>&gt; Hi Stan,\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; I am translating a matlab code to Stan. The goal is to create a exponential decay matrix and \r<br>&gt; apply it to a reward history (either 0 or 1) of some of the subjects, and finally sum them up \r<br>&gt; to get the cumulative reward. Assume my discounting parameter gamma = 0.7. For instance, \r<br>&gt; in the past 5 trials, I have:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; |  0.1681  0.1681  0.1681  0.1681  |       |  1  1  0  0  |\r<br>&gt; |  0.2401  0.2401  0.2401  0.2401  |       |  0  0  1  0  |\r<br>&gt; |  0.3430  0.3430  0.3430  0.3430  |   .*  |  1  0  0  0  |\r<br>&gt; |  0.4900  0.4900  0.4900  0.4900  |       |  1  0  1  1  |\r<br>&gt; |  0.7000  0.7000  0.7000  0.7000  |       |  0  0  1  1  |\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; which leads a new matrix, and what I need is the column sum.\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; In matlab, I could do something like:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;     if t &lt;= 2\r<br>&gt;         cr(t,:) = ones(1,4)*0.25;\r<br>&gt;     else\r<br>&gt;         wght = repmat(gamma.^(t-1:-1:1)&#39;,1,4);\r<br>&gt;         cr(t,:) = sum(wght.*otherReward(1:t-1,:),1);         \r<br>&gt;         cr(t,:) = exp(cr(t,:)) ./ (sum(exp(cr(t,:)))+eps);\r<br>&gt;     end\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; However in Stan, (1) as parameter &#39;wght&#39; changes size every trial,  (2) the power expression (^) does not \r<br>&gt; support vectorization, (3) there is no such a direct way to calculate the column sum. I coded something as below.\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;   for (s in 1:nSubjects) {\r<br>&gt;     \r<br>&gt;     for (t in 1:nTrials) {      \r<br>&gt;       matrix[t-1,4] wgtrew;\r<br>&gt;       \r<br>&gt;       if (t &lt;= 2) {\r<br>&gt;         cr[t] &lt;- initCR;  // [0.25 0.25 0.25]\r<br>&gt;       } else {\r<br>&gt;         for (ct in 1:(t-1)) {\r<br>&gt;           wgtrew[ct] &lt;- (disc[s] ^ (t-ct)) * to_row_vector(otherReward[s,ct]);\r<br>&gt;         }\r<br>&gt;         for (o in 1:4) {\r<br>&gt;           cr[t,o] &lt;- sum( sub_col(wgtrew, 1,o,t-1));\r<br>&gt;         }\r<br>&gt;         cr[t] &lt;- cr[t]/ (sum(cr[t])+machine_precision());\r<br>&gt;       }\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;      ......\r<br>&gt;       \r<br>&gt;     }\r<br>&gt;   }\r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; All the other part remains identical to another alternative model, which runs fairly fast. I doubt it is this piece computing \r<br>&gt; slows down the sampling procedure. \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; I was just wondering if there is a way to optimize it (something comparable in matlab)?\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Hop this post is not so off topic. I appreciate any suggestion or comment.\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Lei\r<br>&gt;  \r<br>&gt; \r<br>"
